    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70089,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intratubular seminoma; intratubular embryonal carcinoma; intratubular trophoblast; intratubular yolk sac tumour; intratubular teratoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70090,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intratubular germ cell tumour types other than GCNIS are occasionally seen adjacent to invasive adult germ cell tumours. Exceptionally rarely, they are seen without invasive disease.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70091,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because intratubular germ cell neoplasia only exceptionally occurs without invasive tumours, there are no specific clinical features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70092,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Insufficient data</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70093,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/36/113\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Tumours of the testis: Introduction</em> .</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70094,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It has not been proved definitively whether these intratubular neoplasms are an intermediate stage between GCNIS and invasive germ cell tumour or merely spread of the invasive tumour down pre-existing tubules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70095,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is not relevant because these tumours are never seen in pure form.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70096,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<h5>Intratubular seminoma</h5>\r\n<p>When GCNIS cells fill the lumina of seminiferous tubules, displacing Sertoli cells, this results in intratubular seminoma. It typically expands the tubules. Like invasive seminoma, it contains lymphocytes. The tubules are often also surrounded by lymphocytic infiltrates. Intratubular seminoma is found in about 30% of seminomas and 15% of non-seminomatous tumours. The cells show an identical immunophenotype to that of seminoma (positive for OCT4, PLAP, and KIT) {12752231; 17592271; 16564921}.</p>\r\n<h5>Intratubular embryonal carcinoma</h5>\r\n<p>Intratubular embryonal carcinoma often shows central necrosis and calcification. Cells are far more pleomorphic than in GCNIS. Differentiation into other components only starts upon invasion {15738984; 30159127}. It is confined to the parenchyma surrounding non-seminomatous tumours and occurs in about 15% of cases {12752231; 17592271}. It is most frequently associated with small tumours, suggesting that larger tumours overgrow and obscure their intratubular precursor {12752231}. Intratubular embryonal carcinoma is positive for CD30, although immature Sertoli cells may also be falsely positive {15279634}. Immature Sertoli cells are also positive for OCT4 but negative for KIT.</p>\r\n<h5>Intratubular trophoblast</h5>\r\n<p>Occasionally, syncytiotrophoblastic cells are seen within tubules. This is mainly associated with seminomas and occurs in 15% of germ cell tumours {16160471}. Intratubular yolk sac tumour and intratubular teratoma have been reported with extreme rarity {30907201}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:26:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
